  "license": "Photograph: © Onyango George / EA Hospitality Pulse, own work. THE LOGO ITSELF IS NOT OURS: the TRA wordmark, device and strapline are trade marks of the Tourism Regulatory Authority, reproduced here for editorial identification of the body the story is about. No endorsement by, affiliation with, or approval from TRA is implied, and no reuse licence in the mark is granted or implied by our credit in the photograph.",
  "licenseurl": "",
  "pin_note": "Supplied by the publisher 18 Sep 2026 and pinned by hand to the Evening Wrap, whose lead is the Senate Committee on Delegated Legislation taking evidence on the Tourism (Tourism Enterprises) Regulations 2026 (Legal Notice No. 127 of 2026) — the licensing, classification and grading regime that TRA administers and enforces. REPLACES a badly mismatched auto-assigned Openverse hero ('Slave Lodge', Jorge Láscar, CC BY 2.0, Cape Town) that had no relationship to the story. TREATMENT: source frame is 532x375; the mark was DOWNSCALED to 592x417 (Lanczos) and centred on a 1200x674 white canvas sampled from the logo's own background, rather than upscaled, so the mark stays crisp and is neither stretched nor distorted — the correct handling for a trade mark. PLACEMENT REVISED on the same day: the mark was first centred, which put the og card's headline block straight across it and left both the strapline and the headline muddy under the teal gradient. It now sits in the upper-right clear zone (382x269 at x=728, y=150) so the headline reads clean and the mark is not obscured. NOTE FOR THE PIPELINE: img/editions/**.jpg is NOT a build-site trigger path, so a hero swap on its own does not regenerate the og card - img/edition-credits.json is a trigger path, which is why this note carries the change. ATTRIBUTION NOTE: the publisher asked to be credited. We credit the PHOTOGRAPH to him, which is accurate, and identify the underlying mark as TRA's, which is also accurate — crediting the logo itself to the publisher would misstate trade-mark ownership. EDITORIAL CAVEAT: a regulator's logo as hero art on a piece critical of that regulator's instrument can read as implying TRA is a source for, or endorses, the analysis. It is not and does not. Swap for a Senate or parliamentary image if that reading becomes a problem.",
